{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1559289-why-is-the-wyrd-so-weak","id":1559289,"title":"Why is the Wyrd so weak?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-04-08T09:14:20-07:00","id":1559289,"title":"Why is the Wyrd so weak?","content":"Something that I noticed when going over the source books: Where are all the really strong characters? I think the most powerful character I saw in all the books (who wasn't a true Fae) had a Wyrd of seven or eight.<br /> The 2nd edition seems to have revised level 10 Wyrd so that not only does it not turn you into a True Fae, but it also makes you a 'fully actualized changeling' according to the Entitlement description. In other words, it's not only no longer something to avoid, but it might even be something that given characters actively want to achieve.<br /> So, if level ten Wyrd is now viewed as fully achievable, why don't the source books give us more characters who have ten Wyrd and attributes up to ten? Why not show us the limits of what these characters are really capable of at their peak, so we know how to describe them accurately?<br /> If someone has ten points in strength, are they like Ajax or Hercules?<br /> If someone has ten points in Presence, is that like Helen of Troy or Medusa?<br /> <br /> I'm sort of surprised how the game never gave us an 'epic tier' for 2nd edition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28466-darkling235","name":"darkling235","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":1,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-04-08T10:04:20-07:00","id":1559291,"title":"","content":"If your threshold for &quot;really strong&quot; is specifically Wyrd 10, that's obviously going to skew your perspective on power; most gamelines put the major players in the 7-10 range of Supernatural Potency and it seems very weird to single out Wyrd when there's not a lot of Blood Potency 10 vampires or Primal Urge 10 werewolves (and so on) on the books, either. (We will, for simplicity's sake, set aside the fact that The Hedge, the last book to be released for Changeling 2e, specifically included an antagonist/failstate whose mechanics include &quot;all of these things have Wyrd 10&quot; in the form of the Geasa).<br /> <br /> If you want advice on superhuman capabilities, other gamelines have various writeups, whether the &quot;Storytelling Transhuman Ability&quot; bit from Imperial Mysteries (late Mage 1e), the &quot;Who I've Come to Be&quot; and &quot;What I've Come to Know&quot; sections from Thousand Years of Night (Vampire 2e), or the plain mechanical effects of the Superhuman [Attribute] Variation in Deviant.<br /> <br /> Paradox being the license-holder for Chronicles and wanting to focus on World has limited the number of new books in general and Changeling, while popular, only has the precedent of being the limited line that broke the limit back when gamelines that weren't Requiem, Forsaken, and Awakening only had a set number of books, period. 2e unfortunately doesn't have the publishing environment necessary to put out new books without going through the Storyteller's Vault like Deviant has with Shallow Graves and Black Vans or None More Dark Studios have done with Book of Seemings, Book of Courts, and their Requiem content.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-17T10:19:43-07:00","id":1560679,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>darkling235</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1559289-why-is-the-wyrd-so-weak#post1559289\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Something that I noticed when going over the source books: Where are all the really strong characters? I think the most powerful character I saw in all the books (who wasn't a true Fae) had a Wyrd of seven or eight.<br /> The 2nd edition seems to have revised level 10 Wyrd so that not only does it not turn you into a True Fae, but it also makes you a 'fully actualized changeling' according to the Entitlement description. In other words, it's not only no longer something to avoid, but it might even be something that given characters actively want to achieve.</div> </div> </div> </div>Simply getting Wyrd 10 didn't make you a True Fae.<br /> <br /> You had to have both <b>Wyrd 10</b>, as well as <b>Clarity 0</b> for that to happen. It represented your character obtaining godly power, while also completely losing their grip on reality.<br /> <br /> Bare in mind that 5 dots is considered to be the pinnacle of what a normal human being is capable of achieving. They include olympic level champions, world renowned scholars, national leaders and religious figureheads. Anything beyond 5 dots is considered to be blatantly supernatural in scale.<br /> <br /> Having 10 in any category puts you in the realm of being among the most powerful beings on the planet, especially if you can further enhance those with your supernatural powers.<br /> <br /> However, it also tends to come with its share of drawbacks. For example, a Vampire who reached Blood Potency of 8-10 can no longer gain sustenance from humans and must feed on vampires to sustain themselves. Obviously, that's going to make integrating with vampire society very difficult.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}